//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/lua-programming/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.6: Enemies and Spawn Timers</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Concept -->
        <section id="concept">
          <h2>The Idea</h2>

          <p>
            Bullets were plain tables, and that was fine because a bullet does
            almost nothing. Enemies will grow behavior &mdash; they move, they
            take damage, later they die in interesting ways &mdash; so they are
            worth making properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">This is Activity 1.11, unchanged.</b>
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure1}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            Four lines you already know: an empty table for the class,{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="Enemy.__index = Enemy"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            to make lookups fall back to it, a constructor that attaches it with{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="setmetatable"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, and methods defined with a colon so they get{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="self"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>What it buys you is the shared-method property.</b> Fifty enemies
            hold fifty positions but there is still exactly one{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="update"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            function. And your main loop gets shorter: it says{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="e:update(dt)"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            and stops caring how an enemy moves.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Notice where the knowledge now lives.</b>{" "}
            The main file no longer knows an enemy's speed or shape. Change{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="Enemy:draw"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            to render a sprite instead of a square and not one line of{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love.draw"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            changes. That is the whole argument for the pattern, and it is what
            makes Activity 2.8 possible.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Spawning on a timer</h3>

          <p>
            The same accumulator you used for the weapon, with a different
            number.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            Starting{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="spawnTimer"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            at <b>0</b> means the first enemy arrives immediately rather than
            after a wait. Start it at{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="spawnEvery"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            instead and the player gets a moment of calm first. Both are
            reasonable; pick deliberately.
          </p>

          <p>
            The spawn x is random but <b>inset from the edges</b> &mdash;{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="math.random(40, width - 40)"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            keeps an enemy from being born half off the screen. And enemies start
            at a <i>negative</i> y so they slide into view rather than popping
            into existence at the top edge.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Seed the generator in{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love.load"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.</b> This is the warning from Activity 1.4 coming due: LÖVE's Lua
            does not seed itself, so without{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="math.randomseed(os.time())"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            every playthrough gets the identical wave pattern. It will look
            deliberate and it is not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Two lists, the same treatment</h3>

          <Code
            code={Figure3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            Identical in shape to the bullet loop, and backwards for the same
            reason. <b>Enemies that reach the bottom are removed</b>, and in a
            finished game that would also cost the player something &mdash;
            Activity 2.9 adds lives.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>An enemy leaving the bottom and a bullet leaving the top are the
            same problem.</b> If you find yourself writing this loop a third
            time, that is the signal to write a helper that takes a list and a
            test. Resist it until then; two is not yet a pattern.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>Same file. You are giving the player something to shoot at.</p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: An Enemy class</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Write the <b>Enemy</b> class with{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="new"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />,{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="update"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              and{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="draw"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              methods.
            </li>
            <li>
              Create <b>two</b> by hand in{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.load"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              so you have something on screen before the timer exists.
            </li>
            <li>
              Update and draw them by calling their methods, not by reaching into
              their fields.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="lua" title="main.lua" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Spawn them on a timer</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Remove the two hand-made enemies.</li>
            <li>
              Add <b>spawnTimer</b> and <b>spawnEvery</b>, and seed the random
              generator in{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.load"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </li>
            <li>Spawn one enemy at a random x every 0.8 seconds.</li>
            <li>
              <b>Run it twice and confirm the pattern differs.</b> Then comment
              out the seed line, run twice more, and watch the exact same wave
              arrive both times.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "8-9, 57-62" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Let them leave</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Turn the enemy loop into a <b>backwards</b> one and remove any
              enemy that has gone off the bottom.
            </li>
            <li>
              Watch the count. <b class="text-sn-theme">It should settle at a
              steady number</b> &mdash; spawning and leaving in balance.
            </li>
            <li>
              Change{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="spawnEvery"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              to 0.2 and watch that steady number roughly quadruple. One variable
              controls the difficulty of the whole game.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "63-64, 66-68" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Give each enemy a <b>random speed</b> as well as a random position,
              so waves stop arriving in a flat line.
            </li>
            <li>
              Make the game get harder: reduce{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="spawnEvery"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              slightly on every spawn, with a{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="math.max"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              floor so it never reaches zero.
            </li>
            <li>
              Add a second enemy type that <b>inherits</b> from Enemy and
              overrides only its speed and color, using the bonus inheritance
              page. Then spawn one at random and note that your update loop needs
              no change at all.
            </li>
            <li>
              Give enemies a gentle side-to-side drift by storing a start x and
              using{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="math.sin"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              of an accumulated time.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Check Your Work</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>Red squares slide down from the top at a steady rate.</li>
            <li>They appear at different horizontal positions each run.</li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">The enemy count rises then holds
              steady</b> instead of climbing forever.
            </li>
            <li>You can still fly and fire; bullets pass straight through.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Bullets ignoring enemies is correct for now &mdash; nothing has been
            told to check. That is Activity 2.7, and it is about twelve lines.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-5">
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-light"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-2/2-5"
          >
            <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Previous
          </a>
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-primary"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-2/2-7"
          >
            Next: Collision Detection <i class="si-arrow-right"></i>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
